{"text": "Lilly’s margin expansion is visible in net margin, but the desk still cannot compute gross margin, operating margin, or the gap between them for LLY or MRK because those inputs are not filed as period series on the desk.

For LLY, net margin over the last 12 quarters was 21.2% on 2023-06-30, -0.60% on 2023-09-30, 23.4% on 2023-12-31, 25.6% on 2024-03-31, 26.2% on 2024-06-30, 8.48% on 2024-09-30, 32.6% on 2024-12-31, 21.7% on 2025-03-31, 36.4% on 2025-06-30, 31.7% on 2025-09-30, 34.4% on 2025-12-31, and 37.4% on 2026-03-31 [f_60b323a506ef]. Gross margin and operating margin were not derivable: the desk said LLY “reports no gross_profit with a period” and “reports no operating_income with a period.” So the gross-minus-operating gap is unavailable from this desk, not zero and not estimated.

On what drove the change, Item 7 gives the cleanest answer. Lilly says: “Revenue increased in 2025 driven primarily by increased volume, partially offset by lower realized prices. The increased volume and lower realized prices in 2025 were primarily driven by Mounjaro and Zepbound.” It also says: “Net income and earnings per share increased in 2025, primarily due to higher gross margin, partially offset by increased marketing, selling, and administrative expenses and research and development expenses.” That means the filing points to volume as the main revenue driver, lower realized prices as the offset, and higher gross margin as the main profit driver, with SG&A and R&D taking some of that away.

So, if you want the answer framed as pricing, mix, or cost, the evidence now supports:
- pricing: lower realized prices were a drag;
- mix/volume: increased volume was the main top-line driver, especially Mounjaro and Zepbound;
- cost: higher gross margin was the main reason net income rose, while marketing, selling, administrative, and R&D expenses partially offset it.

For Merck, the desk is not yet able to compare on the same basis. Merck was put on the desk, but Item 7 was not found and period gross, operating, and net margin could not be computed because MRK “reports no gross_profit with a period,” “reports no operating_income with a period,” and “reports no net_income with a period.” So I cannot say how Merck compares on margin level or expansion yet. What I can say now is only that Merck has been prepared and the comparison is blocked until the desk returns usable filing text or filed series for it.

What would change this reading:
- if the desk can surface Lilly’s gross income and operating income series, the margin gap would show whether expansion came more from gross profit or operating leverage;
- if Merck’s Item 7 and filed period series become available, we can compare whether Lilly’s expansion is stronger, weaker, or just different in mix versus cost;
- until then, the strongest supported interpretation is “volume up, realized prices down, and higher gross margin more than offset by SG&A and R&D,” rather than a pure pricing story."}

